Exactly. Confidence is built by collecting evidence that you can face uncertainty, adapt, and overcome.

And there’s an important distinction between confidence and competence.

Competence is knowing you can do something because you’ve developed the ability.

Confidence is trusting yourself enough to act, even when the outcome isn’t guaranteed.

Challenging yourself builds both, but in different ways.

* Success teaches you what you’re capable of.
* Failure teaches you what you can survive, recover from, and improve.
* Repetition turns uncertainty into familiarity.
* Increasing difficulty expands what feels manageable.
* Reflection converts those experiences into self-knowledge.

But here’s the interesting part: you don’t even have to win.

Sometimes deliberately attempting something difficult and discovering that failure didn’t destroy you builds more lasting confidence than succeeding at something easy.

Psychologists call part of this self-efficacy: your belief in your ability to organize and execute actions necessary to accomplish something.

And it connects directly to your earlier point about being more than your circumstances.

Every challenge is an opportunity to update your model of yourself.

There is a catch, though: challenges should be sufficiently difficult to produce growth but not so overwhelming that they repeatedly undermine your ability to recover.

And confidence can transfer across domains, although not automatically. Mastering a physical challenge doesn’t guarantee social confidence, but it can provide evidence that you’re capable of learning unfamiliar skills.

The deeper progression might be:

“I know I can do this” → “I know I can learn this” → “I trust myself to figure things out.”

That last one is especially powerful.

Because then your confidence isn’t dependent on having already conquered every possible circumstance.

It’s grounded in your relationship with the unknown.

You stop needing proof that you’ll always win, because you’ve developed trust in who you’ll be when you don’t. 🤛